{"name":"Warm Shale","hex":"#b2b1af","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"warm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a somewhat high temperature, often but not always connoting that the high temperature is pleasant rather than uncomfortable.","Friendly and with affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make or keep warm.","To become warm, to heat up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/warm"},{"term":"shale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shell, scale or husk; a cod or pod.","A fine-grained sedimentary rock of a thin, laminated, and often friable, structure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To take off the shell or coat of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shale"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shale","blocks":[{"text":"Shale is a fine-grained, clastic sedimentary rock formed from mud that is a mix of flakes of clay minerals (hydrous aluminium phyllosilicates, e.g., kaolin, Al2Si2O5(OH)4) and tiny fragments (silt-sized particles) of other minerals, especially quartz and calcite. Shale is characterized by its tendency to split into thin layers (laminae) less than one centimeter in thickness. This property is called fissility. Shale is the most common sedimentary rock."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Shale_formation%2C_Marble_Canyon%2C_Kootenay_National_Park%2C_British_Columbia%2C_2025-07-11.jpg/330px-Shale_formation%2C_Marble_Canyon%2C_Kootenay_National_Park%2C_British_Columbia%2C_2025-07-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shale","wikidata":"Q751300"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg/500px-Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg/1280px-Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg/500px-Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sample of drill cuttings of shale while drilling an oil well in Louisiana, United States. Sand grain = 2 mm in diameter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Mudrock_Colors.jpg/500px-Mudrock_Colors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Mudrock_Colors.jpg/1280px-Mudrock_Colors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Mudrock_Colors.jpg/500px-Mudrock_Colors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Color chart for shale based on oxidation state and organic carbon content","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMudrock_Colors.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Shale_in_Potokgraben.jpg/120px-Shale_in_Potokgraben.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Shale_in_Potokgraben.jpg/250px-Shale_in_Potokgraben.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Shale_in_Potokgraben.jpg/120px-Shale_in_Potokgraben.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShale_in_Potokgraben.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/MesselShaleSplitting.JPG/120px-M